          "id": "madronich_cloudy_j_mlev59",
          "description": "END-TO-END ON A CLOUDY COLUMN: the 21 photolysis frequencies RADM2 reads, computed from the column state through the mounted cloud-grid, optical-depth and two-stream components, against the WRF-Chem 4.8 em_scm_xy reference (chem_opt = 101, phot_opt = 1; GNU gfortran 13.3 REAL*4; data/eqwefic/chem_scm_phot; fork branch earthsciml-instrumented-chem) at call 1, model level 59 (radiative-transfer level 64 of the 77 active ones). This column has 6 cloudy interfaces and a total cloud optical depth of 54.9, and WRF's subgrid inserted 7 levels; the grid here is those 77 followed by 54 ZERO-THICKNESS padded ones.\nNOTHING about the grid or the radiation field is supplied: the level insertion, the heights, the temperatures, the air densities (including FORTRAN_BUGS B11, reproduced not corrected), the layer columns, the optical depths, the spherical slant paths and the 260-unknown two-stream solve are all computed from the 70-interface input column. Only the cross sections and quantum yields arrive as data.\nTOLERANCE, measured rather than chosen: rel 2e-3. The reference is a single-precision two-stream solve, and on a cloudy column it is only self-determined to about 5.7e-4 in the j-values -- re-running the identical algorithm in emulated float32 moves the answer by that much, which is MORE than the disagreement here. A tighter tolerance would be asserting the reference's own rounding. The clear column, whose solve is better conditioned, is held to 1e-3 in couplings/madronich_column.esm.",
